      <doc xml:space="preserve"
           filename="gtk/gtkenums.h"
           line="1913">The priority of an accessibility announcement.</doc>
      <member name="low"
              value="0"
              c:identifier="GTK_ACCESSIBLE_ANNOUNCEMENT_PRIORITY_LOW"
              glib:nick="low"
              glib:name="GTK_ACCESSIBLE_ANNOUNCEMENT_PRIORITY_LOW">
        <doc xml:space="preserve"
             filename="gtk/gtkenums.h"
             line="1915">The announcement is low priority,
  and might be read only on the user's request.</doc>
      </member>
      <member name="medium"
              value="1"
              c:identifier="GTK_ACCESSIBLE_ANNOUNCEMENT_PRIORITY_MEDIUM"
              glib:nick="medium"
              glib:name="GTK_ACCESSIBLE_ANNOUNCEMENT_PRIORITY_MEDIUM">
        <doc xml:space="preserve"
             filename="gtk/gtkenums.h"
             line="1917">The announcement is of medium
  priority, and is usually spoken at the next opportunity, such as at the
  end of speaking the current sentence or when the user pauses typing.</doc>
      </member>
      <member name="high"
              value="2"
              c:identifier="GTK_ACCESSIBLE_ANNOUNCEMENT_PRIORITY_HIGH"
              glib:nick="high"
              glib:name="GTK_ACCESSIBLE_ANNOUNCEMENT_PRIORITY_HIGH">
        <doc xml:space="preserve"
             filename="gtk/gtkenums.h"
             line="1920">The announcement is of high
  priority, and is usually spoken immediately. Because an interruption
  might disorient users or cause them to not complete their current task,
  authors SHOULD NOT use high priority announcements unless the
  interruption is imperative. An example would be a notification about a
  critical battery power level.</doc>
      </member>
    </enumeration>
